WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.970
Star Wars stands huge in popular culture,
and while opinions always differ, the 2010s

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.960
have given fans moments that made them cheer,
cry, and everything in between.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.380
Here's our breakdown of the best Star Wars
moments of this last decade.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.160
Upon first watching 2015's The Force Awakens,
most fans were asking themselves the same

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.020
question most of the characters struggled
with throughout the film: "Where's Luke?"

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.600
"We simply do not have enough information
to locate Master Luke."

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.710
The Jedi hero was nowhere to be seen for most
of the movie, and it was enough to make you

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.580
wonder if he was going to show up at all.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.820
Luke's eventual portrayal in The Last Jedi
would not be met with universal praise.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.920
But the moment at the end of The Force Awakens
in which Rey finally meets the Jedi master

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.180
is wonderfully done.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.520
After finding all the pieces of Luke's map
and landing on the remote island where Skywalker

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.500
is hiding out, Rey climbs a crest to find
him a hooded, cloaked figure.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.640
As she holds out the lightsaber to him, Luke
throws back his hood and says nothing.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.360
It's a perfect ending that leaves you wanting
more.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.300
You want to know why he's on the island, why
he left the map fragments to be found, and

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.460
whether or not he has any idea what's been
happening in his absence.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.640
Heck, you kind of just want to know what his
voice sounds like these days.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.720
The Force Awakens brought with it the heartbreaking
death of one of the original trilogy's favorite

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.100
heroes, the original scruffy-lookin' nerf-herder
himself, Han Solo.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.060
But before Han proves he hasn't learned the
important lesson of staying off catwalks over

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.280
bottomless shafts, the smuggler returns to
the movie screen exactly as he should.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.860
With Rey and Finn having stolen the Millennium
Falcon while escaping Jakku, they're snatched

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.700
out of space by a pursuer whom they initially
think is affiliated with the First Order.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.640
Instead, it's a fan-favorite duo from the
original trilogy, hauling carnivorous beasts.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.560
Before our younger heroes can do anything
to stop them, Han and Chewie pull the Falcon

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.060
into the hangar of their larger ship with
a tractor beam.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.820
After an ominous series of clangs and bangs,
the two storm onboard the legendary ship with

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.640
guns drawn.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.760
Once they don't see anyone, Han relaxes a
little, smiles, and says "Chewie, we're home."

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.680
It's an awesome moment, particularly for fans
of the original films.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.240
It doesn't come as much of a surprise, as
Han's line was heavily used in the promotional

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.680
lead up to The Force Awakens.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.240
But it's still tough not to cheer at least
a little when he delivers it.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.940
We would be a whole pack of scruffy-lookin'
nerfherders if we didn't mention perhaps the

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.800
most awww-inspiring and merchandise-promising
Star Wars moment of the decade - the big reveal

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.920
of Baby Yoda.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.500
Everything about the series feels like a bleak
Western classic or a bloody samurai epic,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.400
with its hero being a gun-for-hire who takes
work from remnants of the Empire.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.120
Even the character who proves to be one of
Mando's closest allies, the diminutive Kuiil,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.060
helps the bounty hunter in large part to stop
people like Mando from coming to his home.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.900
Once Mando and the killer droid IG-11 fight
their way past the target's guards, it's a

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.770
genuinely jaw-dropping surprise to learn their
target is an infant in a floating egg - a

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.720
member of the late Yoda's mysterious species.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:53.380
The reveal was an absolute master stroke,
and the shot of Baby Yoda's little hand reaching

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.960
for Mando's gloved finger is sure to be burned
into Star Wars' fans memories for years to

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:58.460
come.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.380
With the exception of the digital Princess
Leia cameo, the closest we got to a Jedi hero

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.940
in 2016's Rogue One is the warrior-monk Chirrut
Îmwe.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:11.720
Chirrut is not a Jedi himself, but as a member
of a religious order charged with protecting

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:15.950
the Temple of Kyber, Chirrut understands the
Force better than any of the heroes of Rogue

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:16.500
One.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.700
Shortly after we first meet him, in spite
of his blindness, Chirrut beats the tar out

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.360
of a squad of Stormtroopers harassing Jyn
and Cassian.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:28.300
Throughout the film, he repeats the phrase:
"The Force is with me, and I am with the Force,"

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.250
often to the irritation of his friend, Baze.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.840
It's precisely this phrase that Chirrut repeats
over and over in the climactic scene of the

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.000
film, when he makes it possible for the heroes
to contact the Rebel fleet fighting in orbit.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.020
In spite of the console being on the other
side of a no man's land filled with laser

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.440
fire, Chirrut picks up his staff and repeats
his credo while walking into a storm of gun

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.020
blasts.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.420
Miraculously, he survives the long walk to
the console and hits the right switches, allowing

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:56.819
Bodhi to contact the Rebel fleet and let them
know to take down the Scariff planetary shield

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.760
in order to receive the Death Star plans.

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:03.200
Unfortunately, Chirrut dies moments later
when the console is destroyed, but his sacrifice

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.260
remains one of the most touching and iconic
moments of the film.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.440
One of the best things about Solo: A Star
Wars Story is that it gives us a lot of the

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.440
Millennium Falcon.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.160
Still, for anyone who's seen the original
trilogy, they're just waiting to find out

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.120
when young Han is going to win the thing from
Lando already.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.060
If you've watched The Empire Strikes Back,
then you know Han won the Falcon fair and

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.180
square in a card game from Lando Calrissian.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:25.840
"What have you done to my ship?"

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.040
"Your ship?

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.020
'Ey.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.940
Remember, you lost her to me fair and square."

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.520
Once we find the younger versions of both
scoundrels looking at each other from across

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.980
a card table, we think we're finally about
to see Han's famous win... but we're wrong.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.180
At least, at first.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.420
Han's luck runs out at his first game of sabacc
with Lando, but he joins Beckett's crew anyway.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.460
Lando later leaves Han and his friends high
and dry when it looks like the Marauders are

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.940
about to take them out, but Han doesn't forget
the ship he fell in love with.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.379
In the final moments of the film, Han tracks
Lando down, snatches the card from the sleeve

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.980
gadget Lando thinks no one can see, and truly
beats the rival smuggler fair and square.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.520
It was the perfect note to end Solo on, and
it felt exactly how the card showdown between

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.680
these two reluctant heroes should've played
out.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.640
By the end of The Force Awakens, we've watched
Kylo Ren not only torture and kill innocents,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.779
but we've been forced to witness one of the
most heart-wrenching moments in Star Wars

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.260
- the death of Han Solo at the hands of his
son.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.310
But after killing Han and almost doing the
same to Finn, Kylo Ren gets his just desserts

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:29.260
on the snowy surface of Starkiller Base, when
Rey teaches him exactly what a Jedi is.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.000
Better still, she does it with the Skywalker
lightsaber, a weapon that Kylo claims belongs

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:34.500
to him.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.220
The moment Rey is able to reach deep within
herself and achieve the calm and confidence

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.740
she needs to defeat Han Solo's killer is one
of the most powerful scenes of the new trilogy.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.040
Plus, it's a truly beautiful shot.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.420
It's that much more powerful when you remember
that no one in the audience knew exactly who

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.340
the Jedi in this movie was going to be.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:55.420
There was a lot of smart misdirection going
on into the promotional lead up to The Force

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.060
Awakens.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.840
Most of the trailers and TV spots showed Finn,
not Rey, wielding the blue lightsaber, even

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.160
going so far to feature shots of the short
duel between Finn and Kylo.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.100
Having died toward the end of The Last Jedi,
Luke Skywalker's Force ghost returns in The

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.700
Rise of Skywalker and his entrance is perfect.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:16.140
Believing she's lost any chance of finding
the hidden Sith planet of Exegol and fearing

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:20.789
her potential for turning to the dark side,
Rey swipes Kylo Ren's TIE fighter and flies

00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:25.440
to Ahch-To, the planet where she finally discovered
the disillusioned Luke Skywalker in the closing

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:26.860
moments of The Force Awakens.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.840
After she intentionally crashes the craft,
the TIE fighter is being destroyed in a raging

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.240
fire when Rey takes out Luke's old lightsaber.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.560
She hurls it at the fire, only to see Luke's
hand reach out and grab the weapon.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.080
It's a powerful call back to the early scene
in The Last Jedi when, after being offered

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.400
his old lightsaber by Rey, Luke flings it
behind him like garbage.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.619
Now, in the afterlife, Luke's clearly got
a different perspective.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.679
If we still don't get the reference, Luke's
Force ghost drives the point home by telling

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.879
Rey that, quote, "a Jedi's weapon deserves
more respect."

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.440
And in case the scene doesn't already have
fans applauding, Luke uses the Force to lift

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.180
his submerged X-wing out of the water so Rey
can use it get to Exegol.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:08.560
We're immediately reminded of a similar act
that he fails at in The Empire Strikes Back,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.400
earning him a hard lesson from Yoda.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:15.179
At the end of The Rise of Skywalker, Rey finds
herself physically alone in her confrontation

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:19.020
with Emperor Palpatine, but in every other
way, she's got plenty of company.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.520
Just like we found her chanting in the beginning
of the film, Rey repeats the phrase "be with

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.640
me," and finally, she gets an answer that
reaches back to every corner of the Skywalker

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:28.140
saga.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.020
The filmmakers gather an army of Jedi voices
from the live-action films and the fan-favorite

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.660
animated series, all assuring Rey they're
with her.

00:07:35.969 --> 00:07:39.440
You might not be able to distinguish them
all from one another, but the credits confirm

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:44.240
that from the live-action trilogies we hear
Samuel L. Jackson as Mace Windu, Frank Oz

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.279
as Yoda, Ewan McGregor as Obi-Wan Kenobi,
Hayden Christensen as Anakin Skywalker, and

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.840
Liam Neeson as Qui-Gon Jinn.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.660
From the animated series The Clone Wars and
Star Wars Rebels, the chorus includes Angelique

00:07:55.669 --> 00:08:01.499
Perrin as Adi Gallia, Ashley Eckstein as Ahsoka
Tano, Freddie Prinze Jr. as Kanan Jarrus,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.880
Jennifer Hale as Aayla Secura, and Olivia
D'Abo as Luminara Unduli .

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.980
If anything was ever meant to be a love letter
to Star Wars fans, this is it.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.280
Bringing together so many voices for a single
scene is something no one expected.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.180
And the chorus of Jedi ends with Rey using
Luke and Leia's crossed lightsabers - as well

00:08:19.189 --> 00:08:23.710
as the power of all the Jedi who came before
her - to bring a satisfyingly final destruction

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:24.700
to Emperor Palpatine.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:29.280
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story isn't a movie
with a lot of lightsaber duels.

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:34.310
There are no colorful Sith/Jedi clashes and
no conflicting politics within the Jedi Council.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:39.420
Regardless, Darth Vader makes two cool appearances,
perhaps most memorably in a hallway slaughter

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:43.450
scene where he boards the Rebel command ship
and relentlessly cuts down the troops who've

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.180
just successfully uploaded the Death Star
plans.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:50.000
The plans survive the hallway attack, but
none of the Rebels in the hallway do.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.940
Most of the Rebels in the following hallway
don't live, either.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.160
One finally races through the airlock of the
Tantive IV, which just barely has time to

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.180
detach with Princess Leia on board.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.160
Ironically, while Vader doesn't feature much
in Rogue One, this scene is arguably the single

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.790
most brutal Vader scene in the history of
Star Wars.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.360
And, if you can believe it, there's an even
more violent version of the scene that you

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:11.380
can find on the Blu-ray.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.180
In a way, this was a scene Darth Vader very
much needed.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.220
After the three prequels, which were filled
with masterfully choreographed lightsaber

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.240
duels, the comparatively stiff, lumbering
Darth Vader can sometimes make you wonder

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.700
how he was ever a threat.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.240
The Rogue One hallway scene reminds you of
exactly what this monster is capable of.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:34.180
Carrie Fisher tragically died one year before
the release of The Last Jedi - a fact that

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:36.960
can't help but lend heartbreaking power to
certain scenes.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:41.420
Fans and critics may always be divided over
the quality of the film, but regardless of

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:45.610
feelings about the movie, you've got to have
some heartstrings pulled when Luke tells Leia,

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:49.380
"No one's ever really gone" before he kisses
her tenderly on her forehead.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.420
The moment is a bit of serendipity.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:52.200
It wasn't planned.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:53.600
At least least the kiss wasn't.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:58.040
Mark Hamill told Entertainment Tonight that
he spontaneously decided to do it in the middle

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:58.600
of the take.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.460
"It just happened.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.990
I was standing up and they weren't ready for
it, and they said, 'No do that again,' and

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:05.880
we did it for a second take."

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.440
Hamill said the scene was, quote, "really
momentous" for him, because it represented

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.360
Luke saying goodbye to his sister forever.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.880
Unfortunately, Hamill lost his friend Fisher
before the scene was shown in theaters.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.360
The actor said that because of the loss, he
still can't watch the scene.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.140
"She'll be forever missed.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:23.980
And she's irreplaceable."

